"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 7.8 and an elevated CAPRA-S score of 9.0. Significant adverse findings include pathological T3b disease with extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, and the presence of lymphovascular invasion. A notable countervailing finding is the absence of documented lymph node metastasis. Furthermore, there is a discrepancy between biopsy findings (Gleason 3+4) and the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+5). The prognosis remains uncertain due to missing postoperative PSA data and unknown follow-up trajectory. Future information regarding the postoperative PSA level and its subsequent pattern would be critical for refining this assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 38.08 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 30.47–45.70 months; its coverage has not been validated."
